-//! Template authorship: a gateway's signed claim, inside its own coinbase, that it built the
-//! block template. R&D sketch for P-003 A2 ([[Tasks/XBT-051]]); nothing in Prime calls it yet.
+//! Sovereign TIDES template attestations ("LZT1"). R&D sketch for P-003 A2 ([[Tasks/XBT-051]]),
+//! following the research note's C.2 #2; nothing in Prime calls it yet.
 //!
-//! The gateway adds one zero-value output to each coinbase variant it publishes:
+//! Per job (per coinbase variant), the gateway signs an attestation `A` with its key `G` and
+//! puts a 70-byte commitment in that coinbase:
 //!
 //! ```text
-//! OP_RETURN OP_PUSHDATA1 100 | "XTA" 0x01 | ed25519 pk (32) | signature (64)
+//! OP_RETURN PUSH68 | "LZT1" | BLAKE2b-256(A) | G
 //! ```
 //!
-//! The signature is by `pk` over [`message`]:
+//! 70 bytes fits RDTS, which caps a coinbase OP_RETURN scriptPubKey at 83 bytes by consensus
+//! (Knots 29.4.2 refuses 84 with `bad-txns-vout-script-toolarge`; XBT-051 regtest). A full
+//! 64-byte signature plus a 32-byte key does not fit, so `A` and its signature travel off chain:
+//! the gateway sends them to Prime with the job, and the pool publishes them for every block.
+//!
+//! `A` is fixed-layout (see [`Attestation::encode`]):
 //!
 //! ```text
-//! "XBT-TEMPLATE-AUTHOR/1" 0x00 | height (u32 LE) | prev hash (32, internal order)
-//! | template root (32) | outputs digest (32)
+//! v (1) | G (32) | height (u32) | prev_hash (32) | branches_hash (32) | coinbase_value (u64)
+//! | n_tx (u32) | mempool_digest (16) | node_tag (16) | t_gbt (u64) | payout_digest (32)
 //! ```
 //!
-//! * The **template root** is the block's merkle root with the coinbase txid replaced by 32 zero
-//!   bytes: it commits to every other transaction and their order, and it is what the gateway's
-//!   stratum merkle branches fold to from a zero leaf. A public verifier gets it from the block's
-//!   txids; Prime gets it from the job's branches. The coinbase is left out because it holds
-//!   the signature.
-//! * The **outputs digest** is SHA256d over the coinbase outputs, serialized as in a transaction
-//!   (count, then value and script each), with the commitment output itself left out. It pins the
-//!   payout split and the segwit witness commitment (hence the wtxids) to the same key.
+//! * `branches_hash` is BLAKE2b-256 over the job's stratum merkle branches, concatenated. Prime
+//!   compares it byte for byte with the job; a public verifier rebuilds the branches from the
+//!   block's txids. It commits to every non-coinbase transaction and their order.
+//! * `payout_digest` (added to the note's sketch) is SHA256d over the coinbase outputs other than
+//!   the commitment. It stops a pool lifting a gateway's `A` onto a coinbase that pays someone
+//!   else: the template can be copied, the payout cannot.
+//! * `mempool_digest`, `node_tag` and `t_gbt` are what the gateway says about its own node; they
+//!   feed the template-entropy detector and are not checked here.
 //!
-//! What a valid claim proves: whoever holds `pk` approved exactly this transaction set and payout
-//! on this parent, before the block existed. It does not prove whose node produced the set; see
-//! the design note for what that leaves open.
+//! `G` is the gateway's DATUM identity key (ed25519), the key the session already handshook with,
+//! so "the attestation's key is this session's key" costs nothing new. The note has a BIP340 key;
+//! the layout is the same 32 bytes, and swapping the scheme is a crate change. Binding `G` to a
+//! payout address (a BIP322 message signed by that address) happens at registration, outside this
+//! module.
 //!
-//! The key is the gateway's DATUM identity signing key, so Prime can require the claim's key to
-//! be the one the session handshook with. The domain prefix keeps these signatures from ever
-//! being valid handshake or configuration signatures, and the other way round.
+//! What a valid attestation proves: whoever holds `G` approved exactly this transaction set and
+//! payout on this parent, before the block existed. Not whose node chose the set: that is the
